<LegalLayout
  title="Editorial policy"
  label="EDITORIAL POLICY"
  updated="20 Nov 2026"
  description="How Durbeen tests tools before writing about them, how AI is used in the writing, how affiliate links are handled, and how corrections are made."
>
  <p>These are the rules I write by. They are meant to be checked, so they are specific.</p>

  <h2>How I test</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Articles are first-hand. I install the tool, read the code where I can, and try it on a real task. Then I say what happened.</li>
    <li>Verdicts are honest, including the negatives. A review says what did not work.</li>
    <li>If I did not test something myself, the article says so and gives the source.</li>
    <li>Reviews show a "last verified" date, because tools change.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How I use AI</h2>
  <p>
    I use AI assistants to help draft and edit articles from my own notes and tests. I review and edit every article,
    and I am responsible for what is published. An article only says I tested something if I did.
  </p>

  <h2>Affiliate links</h2>
  <p>
    There are no affiliate links at the moment. If that changes, the article is marked and shows a note, and the{' '}
    <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a> explains how. Affiliate links never change a verdict.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If you find a mistake, write to hello@durbeen.io. I will correct it and show an updated date on the article.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
